{%- doc -%}
  Whether a product belongs to a sales channel other than the shop, and so should
  not be shown to a retail shopper anywhere.

  Treat House sells the same kitchen through several doors. Most of the catalogue
  is the shop; the rest belongs to somebody else — ReachDesk the corporate gifting
  platform, wholesale, and named clients with their own branded range, Overture and
  PNC among them. All seventy of those sat in the retail catalogue, where a shopper
  met gift-platform SKUs, case quantities and another company's logo on a treat.

  The channel is read from `custom.internal_vendor`, the field the team fills in
  the admin. Not from the name. The codes are written there too, but one product is
  called `We Miss You 12-PackRD` with no space, and a rule that read names would
  miss it and every typo after it. More to the point, Overture and PNC carry no
  code in their names at all. A field is answered or it is not.

  **Any answer hides it.** The test is that the field is filled, not that it holds
  one of today's four values, so the next client to arrive is handled by filling
  the field and nothing else.

  **Hidden from the retail storefront, not taken off the web.** The product stays
  published and its URL keeps working, because ReachDesk sells by sending links.
  What goes is every place a retail shopper browses: collection grids, search, the
  search dropdown, recommendations and the home rows.

  **Except in the collection its own channel is sold from.** A channel that cannot
  be browsed cannot be bought from, and every one of these is somebody's shop:
  wholesale has `wholesale-page` and `reachdesk-copy`, ReachDesk has `reachdesk`,
  Overture and PNC have theirs. Those handles are listed in
  `settings.channel_open_collections`, and a product listed inside one of them is
  shown as normal.

  A flat list is enough because the collections do not overlap — no wholesale
  product sits in the ReachDesk collection, so there is no need to check that the
  open collection is this product's own channel. The day that stops being true,
  this is the line that has to learn about it.

  The one product this cannot reach, and should not: the gift box the box builder
  offers as packaging is a ReachDesk product, and the builder takes it straight
  from a block setting rather than from a collection or a search, so it is never
  judged here.

  Echoes `hidden` when the product should be left out and nothing when it should
  not, so a caller reads it as a string.

  @param {object} product - The product to judge.
  @param {object} [collection] - The collection being listed, when there is one.

  @example
  {%- capture off_channel -%}{% render 'channel-hidden', product: product, collection: collection %}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- if off_channel != blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign channel = product.metafields.custom.internal_vendor.value | default: product.metafields.custom.internal_vendor

  if channel != blank
    # `render` gives a snippet no globals, so the collection being listed is the
    # one the caller passed and nothing else. Search, recommendations and the home
    # rows pass none, which is right: none of them is anybody's shop.
    assign here = collection.handle | downcase
    assign open_here = false

    assign open_list = settings.channel_open_collections | split: ','
    for entry in open_list
      assign entry_handle = entry | strip | downcase
      if entry_handle != blank and entry_handle == here
        assign open_here = true
      endif
    endfor

    unless open_here
      echo 'hidden'
    endunless
  endif
-%}
